  <div class="cc-section">
    <h2>California-Specific Closing Cost Rules</h2>

    <h3>Documentary Transfer Tax (DTT)</h3>
    <p>California Revenue and Taxation Code Section 11911 authorizes counties to levy a documentary transfer tax of <strong>$0.55 per $500 of value</strong> (equivalent to $1.10 per $1,000). Cities may levy an additional city transfer tax on top of the county rate. The tax is typically based on the full purchase price, not the loan amount — unlike some states that calculate only on the financed portion.</p>
    <p>Transfer tax is <strong>customarily paid by the seller</strong> in California, but this is a negotiable term of the purchase contract — not a statutory requirement. In some transactions, particularly in buyer's markets, buyers may agree to pay it.</p>

    <h3>San Francisco Transfer Tax — Tiered Rates</h3>
    <table class="cc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Sale Price</th><th>Combined Rate (County + City)</th><th>Tax on That Amount</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$0 – $250,000</td><td>$6.80/$1,000 (0.68%)</td><td>Up to $1,700</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$250,001 – $999,999</td><td>$6.80/$1,000</td><td>Up to $6,800 on $1M</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,000,000 – $4,999,999</td><td>$11.25/$1,000 (1.125%)</td><td>$11,250 per $1M</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$5,000,000 – $9,999,999</td><td>$12.50/$1,000 (1.25%)</td><td>$62,500 on $5M sale</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$10,000,000 – $24,999,999</td><td>$15.00/$1,000 (1.5%)</td><td>$150,000 on $10M sale</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$25,000,000+</td><td>$25.00/$1,000 (2.5%)</td><td>$625,000 on $25M sale</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Title Insurance in California</h3>
    <p>California title insurance rates are promulgated (filed with the California Department of Insurance) and are the same across title companies for the base rate — you cannot negotiate the rate itself. However, title companies can compete on endorsements, search fees, and escrow services. The two standard policies are:</p>
    <ul style="color:#475569;padding-left:1.4rem;line-height:1.8;font-size:.95rem;">
      <li><strong>CLTA (California Land Title Association) policy</strong> — standard coverage, protects against recorded title defects only.</li>
      <li><strong>ALTA (American Land Title Association) policy</strong> — extended coverage, protects against survey disputes, unrecorded easements, and other off-record risks. Most lenders require ALTA for the lender's policy.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Title insurance premium is calculated as a percentage of the purchase price, typically around <strong>0.19% – 0.35%</strong> depending on county and coverage level. On a $700,000 purchase, the owner's ALTA policy typically runs <strong>$1,500–$2,450</strong>.</p>

    <h3>California Escrow Fees</h3>
    <p>Escrow companies in California charge a combination of a base fee plus a per-thousand rate. A typical formula used by major California escrow companies:</p>
    <table class="cc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Purchase Price</th><th>Typical Total Escrow Fee</th><th>Buyer's Share (50%)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$400,000</td><td>~$1,200</td><td>~$600</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$600,000</td><td>~$1,500</td><td>~$750</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$800,000</td><td>~$1,800</td><td>~$900</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,000,000</td><td>~$2,200</td><td>~$1,100</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,500,000</td><td>~$3,000</td><td>~$1,500</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Property Tax — Proposition 13 Reassessment</h3>
    <p>When you purchase a California property, it is <strong>reassessed to the full purchase price</strong> under Prop 13. The effective property tax rate across California averages approximately <strong>1.1% – 1.25%</strong> of purchase price (base 1% plus local voter-approved bonds and assessments). This means on a $700,000 purchase you can expect annual property tax of <strong>$7,700 – $8,750</strong> per year — significantly higher than the prior owner may have been paying if they owned for decades.</p>
    <div class="cc-info">💡 At closing, 2 months of estimated property taxes are collected into escrow. With a July closing on a $700,000 purchase (~$8,750/year tax), expect <strong>~$1,458 in prepaid property taxes</strong> collected at closing.</div>

    <h3>Worked Example: $700,000 Home, Los Angeles, Conventional 20% Down</h3>
    <table class="cc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Down Payment (20%)</td><td><strong>$140,000</strong></td><td>Paid at closing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Loan Origination (0.75% of $560k)</td><td>$4,200</td><td>Lender fee</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Appraisal</td><td>$650</td><td>Lender-ordered</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Title Insurance (lender's, buyer pays)</td><td>$1,400</td><td>ALTA lender policy</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Owner's Title Insurance</td><td>$0</td><td>SoCal — seller pays by custom</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Escrow Fee (buyer's 50%)</td><td>$875</td><td>~$1,750 total split 50/50</td></tr>
        <tr><td>County Transfer Tax (seller pays)</td><td>$0</td><td>Buyer's share = $0 by custom</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LA City Transfer Tax (seller pays)</td><td>$0</td><td>Buyer's share = $0 by custom</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Home Inspection</td><td>$500</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Prepaid Property Tax (2 months)</td><td>$1,458</td><td>Based on ~$8,750/yr</td></tr>
