  <div class="cc-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions — California Closing Costs</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["What are typical closing costs in California?", "California buyer closing costs typically range from 1% to 3% of the purchase price excluding down payment. On a $700,000 home expect $7,000–$21,000. The largest buyer-paid variable items are lender origination fees, escrow fees (buyer pays half), and title insurance (buyer pays in NorCal)."], ["Who pays transfer tax in California?", "Transfer tax is customarily paid by the seller in California, though this is negotiable. The county rate is $1.10 per $1,000. Many cities add their own tax: Los Angeles city adds $4.50 per $1,000 on all sales; Oakland adds up to $15 per $1,000; San Francisco uses a tiered rate starting at $6.80 per $1,000."], ["Does California require an attorney at closing?", "No. California is an escrow state — a licensed escrow officer at a title company or independent escrow firm handles the closing. No attorney is required, which saves $500–$1,500 compared to attorney-required states."], ["What is the Los Angeles Measure ULA mansion tax?", "Measure ULA (effective April 2023) imposes an additional 4% transfer tax on LA City sales between $5M and $10M, and 5.5% on sales over $10M. This is separate from the standard $5.60/$1,000 combined county/city rate. On a $6M sale, Measure ULA adds $240,000 — making LA one of the most expensive transfer tax jurisdictions nationally."], ["Who pays title insurance in California?", "It depends on the region. In Southern California (LA, Orange, San Diego counties) the seller customarily pays for the owner's title insurance policy. In Northern California (SF Bay Area, Alameda, Santa Clara) the buyer typically pays for the owner's policy. The lender's title insurance is always paid by the buyer in both regions."], ["How does Prop 13 affect closing costs?", "Prop 13 directly affects prepaid property taxes. When a California property sells, it is reassessed to the full purchase price. Your first-year tax bill will reflect the new purchase price at approximately 1.1%–1.25% effective rate — potentially much higher than the prior owner's bill. At closing you typically prepay 2 months of this reassessed amount."], ["What escrow company should I use in California?", "Major California escrow and title companies include First American, Fidelity National Title, Chicago Title, Stewart Title, and Old Republic. In SoCal, the seller's agent often recommends a title/escrow company; in NorCal, the buyer can select. Shopping escrow companies is worthwhile — service fees vary though the underlying title insurance rate is state-regulated."], ["Are California closing costs higher than the national average?", "For buyers, California closing costs are moderate — the main savings are that sellers pay transfer taxes and (in SoCal) owner's title insurance by custom. However, because California home prices are among the highest in the nation, even a modest 1–2% in fees produces large absolute dollar amounts. Lender fees, escrow, and prepaid taxes scale with the purchase price."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
